screen without fork(): Linux compiled to WebAssembly has no MMU, so fork() does not exist
(unistd.h does not even declare it). screen forks in five places; none of them needs a copy of
the process, only a child that execs:

- the backend (screen.c): the attacher forks it and the child carries on in main(). Here the
  backend is screen itself again, started (posix_spawn of /proc/self/exe) with the same
  arguments and SCREEN_NOFORK_BACKEND set, which makes it take the child's side at that point;
  it is still the attacher's child, as before.
- a window's process (window.c), the blanker (display.c), and the printcmd and backtick pipes
  (fileio.c): SpawnChild (misc.c) runs the child's half the way musl's posix_spawn does, with
  clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK) on a stack of its own, so it can still setsid, take the pty
  as its controlling terminal (TIOCSCTTY, which posix_spawn cannot) and set its modes before
  exec. Everything the child needs is worked out first, in the parent, because the child runs
  in the parent's memory; when it cannot exec it says why (ChildFail) and the parent shows it
  with Msg(), where a forked child sent the same message to the backend over the socket.

